      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "STUDY OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To evaluate the antiemetic and analgesic effects of dexamethasone for the first 24 hours postoperatively in patients scheduled for dental surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Prospective, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "University hospital."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "One hundred twenty ASA physical status I and II patients (45 men, 75 women; aged 17-48 years) undergoing general anesthesia for dental surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "Patients received intravenously placebo or dexamethasone at 3 different doses (4, 8, or 16 mg) at the end of the surgical procedure. A standard general anesthetic technique, including sevoflurane and nitrous oxide in oxygen, was used."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "MEASUREMENT AND MAIN RESULTS",
          "content": "Emetic episodes and analgesic requirements were evaluated. The rate of patients who were emesis free (no nausea, retching, or vomiting) during 0 to 24 hours after anesthesia was 63% with dexamethasone 4 mg (P = NS), 83% with dexamethasone 8 mg (P < .05), and 90% with dexamethasone 16 mg (P < .05), compared to placebo (60%). The need for diclofenac sodium for intolerable pain was less in patients who had received dexamethasone 8 mg or dexamethasone 16 mg than in those who had received placebo or dexamethasone 4 mg (P < .05). No clinically important adverse events were observed in any group."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Prophylactic dexamethasone 8 mg is effective for the prevention of nausea and vomiting after dental surgery and in the management of postoperative pain. Increasing the dose to 16 mg provides no further benefit."
        }
      ]},
